{
  "dom_id": "step-e4085",
  "name": "Example: Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Quotient Rules of Logarithms",
  "problem": "Express $\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y)$ as a single logarithm.",
  "worked_solution": "Using the product rule for natural logarithms, we can combine the first two terms of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for natural logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\ln(2x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln \\left( \\dfrac{2x}{y} \\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{y}\\right).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
